<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-analytical-lecture> {
  ex:al-opening a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-analytical-distributions ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-opening-h, ex:al-opening-c ; cd:narration "This lecture connects five distribution families to analytical questions. First establish a common language: density, cumulative probability and inverse CDF. Then ask which quantity varies, why a model is plausible, which assumptions make a reference exact, and how to use its formula with numbers. Normal and lognormal can describe measurement quantities. Chi-square, t and F arise from statistics calculated from samples. Finally check distributional fit and use distributions as inputs to a live uncertainty-propagation simulation. All numerical data are constructed teaching examples, not monitoring evidence."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-opening-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Distributions as tools for analytical questions"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-opening-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-opening-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-opening-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-overview ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-functions a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:cumulative-distribution-function ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-functions-h, ex:al-functions-v ; cd:narration "Use the same simple uniform model from zero to one in all three panels. It is an abstract dimensionless input, not specifically concentration. In the PDF, horizontal position is a possible value and vertical height is density per value unit; shaded area is probability. In the CDF, horizontal position is a value threshold and vertical height is cumulative probability up to it. In the inverse CDF, swap the roles: horizontal input is a probability and vertical output is its quantile value. Equal numerical coordinates in this uniform example do not make the axes interchangeable. We now calculate one rectangle probability, the same CDF difference and one quantile. Later the same uniform inputs will drive Monte Carlo. CDFs also apply to discrete models, unlike an ordinary continuous density."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-functions-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "One model, three ways to ask about it"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-functions-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-functions-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-functions-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-functions-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-density a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:probability-density-function ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-density-h, ex:al-density-v, ex:al-density-f ; cd:narration "The horizontal axis shows the value x; the vertical axis is density, not probability. Here a uniform input from zero to one has constant density one within that interval and zero outside. Its total rectangle area is one. This is a teaching model for a simulation input, not a claim that analytical measurement errors are uniform. Density is measured per value unit; if x had physical units, density would have reciprocal units. Integrating density over value therefore gives dimensionless probability. Choosing density zero rather than one at an endpoint changes no probability. Exact single points still have zero probability. Next calculate an interval by multiplying its width by the density height."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-density-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "PDF: probabilities are areas, not heights"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-density-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-density-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-density-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-uniform-pdf-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-density-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-pdf ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-area-example a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:probability-density-function ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-area-h, ex:al-area-v, ex:al-area-f ; cd:narration "For a Uniform(0,1) simulation input, calculate the probability of a value between 0.25 and 0.75. Read those bounds on the horizontal value axis. The width is 0.50 and density height is one per value unit, so rectangle area is 0.50, or 50 percent. Height one does not mean 100 percent probability at one exact value; width is essential. The whole support has width one and total probability one. We next get exactly the same interval probability from two CDF heights. This is a model probability, not a confidence interval for an unknown mean."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-area-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Example: calculate the shaded probability"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-area-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-area-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-area-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-uniform-pdf-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-area-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-area-work ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-cumulative a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:cumulative-distribution-function ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-cumulative-h, ex:al-cumulative-v, ex:al-cumulative-f ; cd:narration "The horizontal axis is still the value threshold x, but the vertical axis has changed from density to cumulative probability F(x). For Uniform(0,1), F is zero below zero, increases linearly across the support and remains one above one. At 0.25 its height is 0.25; at 0.75 its height is 0.75. Subtract these vertical probabilities to get 0.50, matching the shaded PDF rectangle. Do not calculate area under the CDF. Here the plotted segments are exact, not an approximation to a curved model. A CDF also exists for discrete laws, where it jumps; an inclusive lower endpoint then needs the left limit instead of F at that endpoint."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-cumulative-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "CDF: accumulate, then subtract"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-cumulative-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-cumulative-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-cumulative-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-uniform-cdf-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-cumulative-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-cdf ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-quantile a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:theoretical-quantile ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-quantile-h, ex:al-quantile-v, ex:al-quantile-f ; cd:narration "Now plot the inverse function with its own axes: horizontal is the requested probability u, vertical is the returned quantile value Q(u). In the previous CDF plot we read a value to obtain a probability; here we start with probability to obtain a value. For Uniform(0,1), input 0.75 returns value 0.75. Equal numbers are a special property of this model, not a universal identity between probability and measured value. For a physical quantity the output has that quantity's units while input probability remains dimensionless. The plot endpoints illustrate one-sided limits; our definition uses probabilities strictly between zero and one. The generalized inverse handles jumps and is not reciprocal 1/F. Later critical values and Monte Carlo input generation reuse this operation."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-quantile-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Inverse CDF: probability in, threshold out"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-quantile-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-quantile-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-quantile-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-uniform-inverse-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-quantile-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-inverse ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-normal-story a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-normal-story-h, ex:al-normal-story-c ; cd:narration "An analyst repeats nitrate measurements on one collected homogeneous sample. Results differ even though the sample is unchanged. Many small additive contributions can motivate a normal error model, but common bias, drift, correlation or outliers require separate attention. The existing five constructed values 9.6 through 10.4 mg/L illustrate this laboratory situation; they do not establish normality. For the next model calculations specify mean 10 and SD 0.30 mg/L as teaching parameters, not estimates fitted from those five values. We ask about analytical variation, not the environmental representativeness of the collected sample."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-story-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Normal: repeated measurements of one sample"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-story-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-normal-story-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-story-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-normal-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-model a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-normal-model-h, ex:al-normal-model-v, ex:al-normal-model-f ; cd:narration "The preceding process picture motivated a candidate normal model. Read the first line as X follows a normal distribution: the calligraphic capital N names the family, the tilde means distributed as, the first parameter is mean mu and the second is variance sigma squared, not standard deviation. Here the mean is 10 mg/L and SD 0.30 mg/L. The density formula describes that same curve; it normalizes area to one and has reciprocal concentration units. We do not evaluate its height as an isolated analytical objective. It is the shape whose areas answer probability questions. The tails continue beyond the drawing, and normal support on the real line needs reconsideration near physical or detection limits. Next introduce the laboratory control question, then answer it directly with this model's CDF. Standardization is a separate reusable tool after the complete worked case."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-model-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Normal model for analytical results"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-model-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-normal-model-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-model-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-n09a-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-model-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-normal-law ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-density-example a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-normal-density-h, ex:al-normal-density-v, ex:al-normal-density-f ; cd:narration "Insert value 10, mean 10 and SD 0.30 mg/L into the normal density. Their difference is zero, so the exponential is one. The remaining reciprocal normalization gives 1.3298076 L/mg. This is a useful formula evaluation, not a probability of 133 percent. The probability at exactly 10 is zero in the continuous model. To answer an analytical threshold question we need an area, obtained from a CDF on the next slide."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-density-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Example: evaluate the density, not a probability"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-density-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-normal-density-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-density-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-n09a-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-density-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-normal-work ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-probability a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-normal-probability-h, ex:al-normal-probability-v, ex:al-normal-probability-f ; cd:narration "Apply the laboratory control story directly in concentration units, using the CDF concept already introduced. Specify mean 10.0 and SD 0.30 mg/L to a numerical normal CDF, for example pnorm in R. At threshold 10.45 mg/L it returns cumulative probability 0.9331928. Subtract from one to obtain upper flag probability 0.0668072, about 6.68 percent. No new standardized random variable is needed for this calculation. Thus an in-control process under this model still triggers the one-sided rule about 6.68 percent of the time. A flag does not prove failure, and this is not a legal or safety judgement. Software evaluates the normal integral; the full model tail, not only the finite drawing, determines the result. Next reverse the CDF to choose a five-percent upper flag rate."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-probability-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Control example: quantify the upper review-flag rate"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-probability-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-normal-probability-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-probability-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-n10a-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-probability-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-control-probability-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-inverse a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:theoretical-quantile ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-normal-inverse-h, ex:al-normal-inverse-v, ex:al-normal-inverse-f ; cd:narration "Keep the same in-control nitrate model and choose a five-percent upper review-flag rate. This means cumulative probability 0.95 below the threshold. Supply probability 0.95, mean 10.0 and SD 0.30 mg/L to the normal inverse CDF, for example qnorm in R, obtaining 10.493456 mg/L. This directly reuses the inverse-CDF principle without assuming knowledge of standardization. Under the model it leaves five percent above the threshold. It is a one-sided individual-result rule, not a central 95 percent interval, a mean confidence interval or a fully validated quality-control procedure. This plot is a CDF: horizontal concentration threshold, vertical cumulative probability. The inverse calculation reads the desired probability back to a threshold. After finishing this case, introduce standardization as a separate coordinate tool for the later statistical constructions."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-inverse-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Control example: choose a 5% upper flag rate"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-inverse-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-normal-inverse-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-inverse-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-n10b-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-inverse-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-control-inverse-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-log-story a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:lognormal-concentration-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-lognormal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-log-story-h, ex:al-log-story-c ; cd:narration "Change the mechanism explicitly. Preparation recovery and dilution can act as multiplicative factors on a positive concentration. If a product of such factors has an approximately normal natural logarithm, a lognormal model can be appropriate. This is not a consequence of positivity alone, and it is not the distribution of particle counts merely because particles are physical objects. Treat zeros, blank corrections and censored results separately. We use a new constructed concentration model with reference 1 mg/L, log mean 0.69315 and log SD 0.75. These log parameters are dimensionless and are not the preceding normal mean and SD."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-story-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Lognormal: multiplicative concentration variation"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-story-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-log-story-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-story-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-log-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-model a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:lognormal-concentration-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-lognormal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-log-model-h, ex:al-log-model-v, ex:al-log-model-f ; cd:narration "Divide concentration by the fixed reference before taking its logarithm, so the logarithm acts on a dimensionless ratio. A normal model for that log ratio defines a lognormal concentration. The twenty constructed observations illustrate the scale change; the superimposed curve has specified parameters and is not a fit demonstrating normality. For example concentration 2 mg/L gives log ratio about 0.693147, close to the specified log mean. Next use these parameters to calculate two different population summaries on the original scale."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-model-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Normal log ratios, lognormal concentrations"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-model-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-log-model-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-model-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-l12b-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-model-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-l12b-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-example a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:lognormal-concentration-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-lognormal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-log-example-h, ex:al-log-example-v, ex:al-log-example-f ; cd:narration "Insert reference 1 mg/L, log mean 0.69315 and log SD 0.75. Exponentiating the log mean gives about 2.00 mg/L, the population median and geometric mean. For arithmetic expectation, add half the log variance in the exponent: 0.75 squared divided by two is 0.28125, giving about 2.65 mg/L. Thus back-transforming a log centre does not recover the arithmetic mean. These are model summaries, not estimates calculated from the twenty illustrative values. A right-skewed positive model changes which summary answers the analytical question."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-example-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Example: median 2.00, mean 2.65 mg/L"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-example-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-log-example-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-example-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-l12c-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-log-example-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-log-work ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-chi-story a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-chi-story-h, ex:al-chi-story-c ; cd:narration "A laboratory estimates repeatability variance from five measurements. Another five-measurement series produces a different variance even if the method is unchanged. The illustrated cards identify the analytical study, the variable of interest and the purpose of a reference distribution. We now study the variance estimate, not an individual concentration. Under independent identically normal sampling its scaled distribution is chi-square. Its quantiles let us quantify uncertainty in the precision estimate itself. The next slide introduces that reference; the worked example then returns to the five constructed laboratory results and their observed variance 0.10 in squared concentration units."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-story-context a cd:Interpretation ; cd:body "**Same method, different variance estimates.** Each bar summarizes five simulated laboratory measurements. Estimated precision also has uncertainty."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-story-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-chi-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-story-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Chi-square: precision is estimated too"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-story-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-chi-story-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-story-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16a-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-model a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-chi-model-h, ex:al-chi-model-v, ex:al-chi-model-f ; cd:narration "For iid normal measurements, sample size minus one times random sample variance divided by population variance is chi-square with sample size minus one degrees of freedom. Fitting the mean leaves that many independent normal residual coordinates; their squared standardized sum gives this law. The centered raw residuals are not themselves independent. With five measurements the reference has four degrees of freedom. Inserting observed variance 0.10 and hypothesized population variance 0.10 in squared concentration units gives observed scaled value four, but the true population variance is normally unknown. Invert the chi-square quantiles to obtain its interval next. This exact variance law is not the same application as Pearson's approximate goodness-of-fit statistic."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-model-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Normal measurements imply a chi-square variance reference"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-model-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-chi-model-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-model-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16b-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-model-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16a-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-example a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-chi-example-h, ex:al-chi-example-f ; cd:narration "Use the same five constructed laboratory results 9.6, 9.8, 10.0, 10.2 and 10.4 mg/L. Their squared deviations sum to 0.40, and dividing by four gives sample variance 0.10. Inverse CDF of chi-square with four degrees of freedom gives 0.025 and 0.975 quantiles 0.4844186 and 11.1432868. Divide 0.40 by the larger quantile for the lower endpoint and by the smaller quantile for the upper: approximately 0.03590 to 0.82573 in squared concentration units. The wide interval shows how little five values constrain variance. Under iid normal sampling the procedure has 95 percent coverage for analytical variance. It is neither a concentration interval nor a probability assignment to a fixed variance after seeing the data. It excludes environmental sampling and systematic bias."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-example-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Example: a variance interval"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-example-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-chi-example-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-chi-example-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-chi-work ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-t-story a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-t-story-h, ex:al-t-story-c ; cd:narration "The analyst now reports the mean of the same five measurements. The population SD is unknown and is estimated from those same values. That adds denominator uncertainty to the standardized mean. Under a normal model, the mean is independent of sample variance, giving the exact Student-t reference. This is why replacing population SD by sample SD does not leave a standard-normal statistic. Our target is the common analytical expectation of one collected sample, not a water-body mean and not automatically the unbiased true concentration."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-story-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Student-t: a mean with estimated spread"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-story-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-t-story-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-story-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-t-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-model a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-t-model-h, ex:al-t-model-v, ex:al-t-model-f ; cd:narration "Subtract the candidate analytical expectation from the sample mean and divide by estimated SE: sample SD over the square root of size. Under iid normal sampling this random statistic is t with n minus one degrees of freedom. The density comparison illustrates heavier tails at small degrees of freedom, not a fitted curve from five points. For a hypothetical reference 9.5 mg/L, observed mean 10 and SD square root of 0.10 with size five give a statistic about 3.5355. This is a calculation conditional on a fixed reference, not a claim that its own uncertainty or analytical bias has been accounted for. Next build a two-sided interval using a t quantile."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-model-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Standardize by an estimated standard error"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-model-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-t-model-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-model-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16d-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-model-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16d-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-example a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-ci ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-t-example-h, ex:al-t-example-f ; cd:narration "Insert observed mean 10 mg/L, sample SD square root of 0.10 and size five. Estimated SE is 0.141421 mg/L. The inverse t CDF at 0.975 with four degrees of freedom is 2.776445. Multiplying gives half-width 0.392649 mg/L. Subtract and add it to obtain about 9.60735 to 10.39265 mg/L. Software supplies the quantile; the remainder is transparent arithmetic. Across iid normal laboratory series this procedure covers their fixed analytical expectation 95 percent of the time. It is not a prediction interval, a frequentist probability for the fixed expectation after seeing the interval, or an environmental mean interval. Calibration bias and other uncertainty contributions are not automatically included."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-example-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Example: use the inverse t CDF for a mean interval"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-example-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-t-example-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-t-example-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16g-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-f-story a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-f-distribution ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-f ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-f-story-h, ex:al-f-story-c ; cd:narration "Now compare analytical repeatability of two methods. This is a new hypothetical design, not sample sizes inferred from the earlier incomplete two-SD illustration: six independent normal measurements per method and independent series. Observed SDs are A 0.20 and B 0.40 mg/L. Before looking at results specify a one-sided question: is method B's variance larger. A larger sample SD alone is not proof of a population precision difference or method superiority. Since both variances fluctuate, compare their ratio with an F reference under equal population variances."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-story-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "F: compare two analytical precision estimates"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-story-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-f-story-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-story-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-f-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-model a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-f-distribution ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-f ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-f-model-h, ex:al-f-model-v, ex:al-f-model-f ; cd:narration "With iid normal observations within each method, independent series and equal population variances, the sample variance ratio B over A has an F law. The two ordered parameters are numerator sample size minus one and denominator sample size minus one. For six per method that is five and five. F arises as the ratio of independent chi-square quantities, each divided by its own degrees of freedom. The plotted family curves illustrate shapes, not the fitted distribution of these two methods. Next calculate the observed ratio and the actual five-five reference quantile."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-model-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "An F reference needs two ordered degrees of freedom"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-model-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-f-model-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-model-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-f17b-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-model-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-f17b-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-example a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-f-distribution ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-f ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-f-example-h, ex:al-f-example-f ; cd:narration "Square the SDs before dividing: 0.40 squared over 0.20 squared is four, not two. Under the equal-variance null with five numerator and five denominator degrees of freedom, the inverse F CDF at 0.95 is about 5.050329. Four is below that prespecified one-sided threshold, so do not reject at five percent. This does not establish equivalent precision or accuracy. Reversing the ratio or choosing the larger variance after looking at data would require a different decision procedure; do not silently turn this into a two-sided test. F is sensitive to nonnormality, so the assumptions are part of the conclusion."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-example-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Example: a variance ratio is not automatically significant"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-example-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-f-example-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-f-example-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-f-work ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-test-story a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-kolmogorov-smirnov ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-ks ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-test-story-h, ex:al-test-story-c ; cd:narration "A mechanistic argument motivates a model; it does not prove fit. We now compare data with model expectations. The continuous KS example uses a fully specified normal CDF and a new five-value constructed dataset. Pearson then compares particle-size category counts with prespecified proportions, not a normal sample-variance statistic. Both require justified sampling assumptions and correct reference calibration. Estimating a model's parameters from these same data changes the reference, requiring an appropriate correction or calibrated simulation. Non-rejection is not proof of a distribution, and neither test establishes independence, analytical accuracy or representativeness."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-test-story-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Distribution tests check a model, not its truth"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-test-story-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-test-story-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-test-story-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-tests ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-map a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-analytical-distributions ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-map ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-map-h, ex:al-map-v ; cd:narration "Read this as a map of mathematical constructions, not a rule that all analytical data are normal. A positive lognormal quantity becomes normal after taking the log of its ratio to a reference. Standardize independent normal quantities, square and sum to obtain chi-square. Divide an independent standard normal by the square root of an independent chi-square divided by its degrees of freedom to obtain t. Divide two independent chi-square quantities each scaled by its own degrees of freedom to obtain F. Independence and the scaling are essential. Chi-square and F concern variance-related constructions; t concerns a standardized mean with estimated spread. Next use distributions differently: as uncertainty models for inputs to an analytical measurement equation, propagated by Monte Carlo."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-map-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "How the five distribution families connect"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-map-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-map-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-map-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-map-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
}
